- op: discuss  # b8a3
  at: '2026-09-04T14:51:08Z'  # b8a3
  by: agent  # b8a3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b8a3
  clock: 7  # b8a3
  nonce: b8a3  # b8a3
  message: "DONE in PR #770, and this item's own size estimate is REFUTED by the work — both are part\nof the result.\n\nSHIPPED. Strategy 1.9 already inferred the receiver type of `File::create(p)?.write_all(..)`\nand dropped it when the in-repo `Type::method` lookup missed; the carry is now there, with\nthe qualified path recovered from the producer's scoped callee (the item's ADR-0051\nannotation named exactly this: `std::fs::File`, not the bare `File` the helper returns).\n`?` and `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` ship together because they are ONE mechanism -- a wrapper\nprojecting `Result<T,E>` to `T` -- and the measurement decided that scope: over ripgrep +\nbellman + tiktoken-rs a receiver reached through `.unwrap()` occurs 49 times against 29 for\n`?`, so shipping only the syntax form would have fixed the rarer half.\n\nTWO GUARDS, BOTH FOUND BY READING MOVED ROWS BACK RATHER THAN BY REASONING:\n  (a) the CALL's own callee must not be a projector -- `unwrap`'s receiver is the\n      `Result<File, E>`, not the `File`. Without it, 135 of 213 new ripgrep edges were\n      `unwrap` attributed to `std::fs::File`.\n  (b) the RECEIVER must have been explicitly unwrapped -- `File::create(p)` evaluates to a\n      Result, and nothing here knows whether a library associated function is fallible.\n\nMEASURED, every surviving row read back at source:\n    ripgrep      typed module slots 431 -> 442,  11 new edges,  0 genuine losses\n    bellman      typed module slots 220 -> 233\n    tiktoken-rs  typed module slots  66 ->  67\nripgrep's 11: `file.write_all` x3, `set_len` x2, `lines` x2, `flush`, `h.as_file()` ->\n`winapi_util::Handle`, `sink.stats().unwrap().clone()` -> `crate::stats::Stats`. The 8\nA-only rows are those same sites under `external::` -- re-keys. io-boundaries UNCHANGED\n(ripgrep 30/25; bellman and tiktoken-rs 0/0 as controls).\n\nTHE 580-SITE HEADLINE IS NOT REACHABLE. I sized the associated-fn receiver population at\n580 before building, and guard (b) gives most of it up on purpose: a raw `Type::assoc()`\nreceiver is a Result as often as it is the type. The reachable population is the\nexplicitly-unwrapped forms -- 29 `?` sites plus 49 `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` sites on this\ncorpus. Withholding beats a confident wrong module, but the expected payoff should be\nrestated at that size.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO NEIGHBOURING RECORDS:\n1. `test_rust_field_receiver_module_slot.py`'s shape C was recorded as WI-lalot's, \"because\n   the type is never written down\". The type IS written down -- in the producer's own\n   scoped path -- so the shape closed HERE, with no `std::fs::File::create` row involved.\n   The assertion moved from \"still unclassified\" to \"classifies\", as its own failure\n   message instructed.\n2. INSTRUMENT DEFECT invalidating earlier rust churn numbers: `pair_churn_lang.py` splits a\n   dst on ':' and reads fields POSITIONALLY, which assumes the module has no colons. A rust\n   module does, so a site whose module gains `::` reads as one loss plus one gain. That is\n   why this run first reported 42 ripgrep losses where a file:line read-back finds 0.\n   `slot_count.py` (in the lab) anchors on the span instead. Any rust churn number from\n   that rig is suspect wherever a module is qualified -- including INV-pamis's lab.\n\nLab: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/papar_rust_chained_09042026/ (FINDINGS.md)."  # b8a3
